# Cameo ISO package set.
#
# Versions are pinned per GPU tier during Phase 5; this is the baseline set.
# Confirm the ROCm / kernel / mesa combination against scripts/phase1 results
# before shipping — the whole point of Cameo is a validated combination.

# Base system
base
linux
linux-firmware
linux-headers
mkinitcpio
# Builds the archiso hooks into the initramfs — without it the live image has
# no way to find and mount its own squashfs root.
mkinitcpio-archiso
# Required by the 'bios.syslinux' boot mode; mkarchiso fails profile
# validation outright if it is absent.
syslinux
sudo
# The account database is inherited from releng, which points root at zsh.
# Without it the shell does not exist and tty1 autologin fails silently.
zsh

# AMD graphics + Vulkan userspace (the universal baseline — every tier)
mesa
vulkan-radeon
vulkan-icd-loader
vulkan-tools
# VA-API comes from `mesa` itself — the standalone libva-mesa-driver package no
# longer exists upstream, it is only a virtual provide now.

# ROCm *runtime* for Tier 1/2 serve. Not the HIP SDK and not PyTorch — those
# turn the universal ISO into a 10+ GiB file GitHub Releases will not host
# (2 GiB cap). ggml-hip pulls the HIP runtime it needs; rocminfo/rocm-smi
# are what Cameo uses to detect and enable ROCm. Training stays a later
# install (container / cameo train), not something the USB has to carry.
rocminfo
rocm-smi-lib

# The inference engine itself. llama-cpp provides llama-cli, llama-server and
# llama-quantize -- everything the CLI spawns. Without it the image boots but
# every serve/run/quantize path dies with ENOENT, which is the whole mission.
#
# Upstream consolidated llama-cpp-vulkan and llama-cpp-rocm into this single
# package and moved the compute backend into ggml, which now ships one package
# per backend. That maps onto Cameo's tiers directly: ggml-vulkan is the
# universal baseline for every card, ggml-hip is the Tier 1/2 accelerator and
# is stripped for the lite edition by scripts/build-iso.sh.
llama-cpp
ggml-vulkan
ggml-hip

# Wireless. The target is a laptop, and networkd alone cannot associate to an
# access point -- that is iwd's job. Without this there is no way to fetch a
# model on a machine with no Ethernet port.
iwd
wireless-regdb

# AMD microcode. releng's mkinitcpio config already has the microcode hook;
# without this package there is nothing for it to embed, on an AMD-first distro.
amd-ucode

# Enough to git-clone and curl a GGUF. The compiler toolchain stays off the
# ISO so the image is an appliance, not a 10 GiB dev kit.
git
curl
python

# Minimum usable live system. openssh is present but its daemon is NOT
# enabled -- the client matters for a serving box, the listener is a
# deliberate opt-in.
nano
less
openssh
# An editor and the manuals. A box you are meant to develop on should not
# require a second machine to fix a config file or look up a flag.
vim
man-db
man-pages

# Installer tooling. `cameo-install` runs from this live medium to put Cameo on a
# disk by COPYING the live image (offline — no mirrors), then installing a
# bootloader and creating the admin account. The medium therefore needs:
#   * squashfs-tools — unsquashfs, to unpack the live airootfs onto the target;
#     rsync is the fallback copy path when the pristine sfs is unavailable.
#   * grub + efibootmgr — the bootloader is installed into the COPIED system, so
#     GRUB must already live in the image (it is not fetched at install time).
#   * arch-install-scripts — genfstab/arch-chroot to configure the target.
#   * gptfdisk (sgdisk), dosfstools (mkfs.fat for the EFI partition),
#     parted (partprobe).
arch-install-scripts
squashfs-tools
rsync
grub
efibootmgr
gptfdisk
dosfstools
parted

# NOTE: the `cameo` CLI and the `cameod` control-plane daemon are injected by the
# build pipeline (cargo build --release, staged into the airootfs) rather than
# listed here as packages. cameod is started on boot by cameod.service and serves
# the browser console on 127.0.0.1:9090 by default. See archiso/README.md.
